**Lemma 7.1 (credited actual-tree injectivity and literal separation).**
Substitution is injective on $\mathcal T$; inserting its argument once into a
fixed ordered context is injective. Distinct finite trees differ at some finite
literal address. Consequently, along one common acquired branch, any composite
of accepted substitutions and fixed-context grafts, Reads and rejected attempts
is injective as a map of the initial source to the current source.

Proof. These are existing suppliers, including `GenealogicalFiberTransport.result`
and the ordinary recursive decoder in Process Geometry Theorem 44.6. Explicitly,
no substitution image is the leaf alpha. The image beta comes only from alpha.
The image $\langle\beta,\alpha\rangle$ comes only from beta, because an
internal-node image cannot have right child alpha. Any other node on the actual
image decodes both children recursively. This gives a left inverse on the actual
image and proves injectivity. Constructor injectivity, inducting along the one
hole path, gives the context claim. For literal separation compare roots; if
equal leaf labels, the trees are equal; if both branches, a differing child has
a distinguishing suffix by induction, prefixed by its side. Reads and rejects
are source identities. On a common branch the action sequence and complete
actual contexts are fixed, so these injective maps compose. The decoder does
not authorize an inverse physical operation or a reroot.

